			"date": "2024-03-20T14:27:37.127Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://social.treehouse.systems/@egallager\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>egallager</span></a></span> I think you need to feed the pathprefix into the pagefind CLI here: <a href=\"https://github.com/tweetback/tweetback/blob/bb062375898f5f6e4f338681cdfe8aa9f90e36da/eleventy.config.js#L37\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">github.com/tweetback/tweetback</span><span class=\"invisible\">/blob/bb062375898f5f6e4f338681cdfe8aa9f90e36da/eleventy.config.js#L37</span></a></p><p>This is the CLI param: <a href=\"https://pagefind.app/docs/config-options/#output-subdirectory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">pagefind.app/docs/config-optio</span><span class=\"invisible\">ns/#output-subdirectory</span></a> (although tweetback is using pre-1.0 pagefind)—so you’ll need to investigate if that CLI param was renamed in 1.0 😬</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112128483826373083",
			"parentId": "112128482156627034",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/112128483826373083",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-03-20T14:28:02.606Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://social.treehouse.systems/@egallager\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>egallager</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fosstodon.org/@bglw\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>bglw</span></a></span> might be able to answer this!</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112128500185930937",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@villepreux/112128500161094046",
			"author": {
				"name": "Antoine",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@villepreux",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/368/026/309/762/657/original/affbee841519a642.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-03-20T14:32:11.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> </p><p>Hello Zac.</p><p>I hope you don't mind me responding in private. I'm not a pro webdev so not so confident on thoses issues.</p><p>But, seeing</p><p>\"We strive to meet the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG) 2.1 at the AA level.\"...</p><p>If someone or some organization is really targeting accessibility, should'nt it target APCA contrasted color scores, instead of (or as well as) AA ?</p><p>Or maybe APCA scientific studies on the subject are not valid and it does not improve things?</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112128501273302814",
			"parentId": "112128500185930937",
			"url": "https://mastodon.social/@villepreux/112128501258518259",
			"author": {
				"name": "Antoine",
				"url": "https://mastodon.social/@villepreux",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/368/026/309/762/657/original/affbee841519a642.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2024-03-20T14:32:28.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> </p><p>But if those studies are valid, I do not really understand why flagship structures like ClouldCanon, 11ty etc. are not advocating for it</p><p>APCA not being a standard yet does not seem to be a valid reason for me.</p><p>(And if targeting WCAG AA for some legal reason does not prevent also targeting APCA)</p><p>Let me know. There might be a turnback against APCA or issues I'm not aware of.</p><p>And since you're a web good practices advocate you must be aware of those.</p><p>Thanks</p><p>Best<br>Antoine</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112129028155854916",
			"parentId": "112128501273302814",
			"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat/112129028155854916",
			"author": {
				"name": "Zach Leatherman",
				"url": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/accounts/avatars/109/286/461/031/266/152/original/dc489c3746a44e00.png"
			},
			"date": "2024-03-20T16:46:28.414Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://mastodon.social/@villepreux\" class=\"u-url mention\">@<span>villepreux</span></a></span> good feedback! I think this is worth talking about—I’ll run it by some folks internally</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112129693956619809",
			"parentId": "112128483826373083",
			"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@bglw/112129693930497728",
			"author": {
				"name": "Liam Bigelow",
				"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@bglw",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/343/937/842/242/416/original/8905219901a4a606.jpeg"
			},
			"date": "2024-03-20T19:35:47.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://social.treehouse.systems/@egallager\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>egallager</span></a></span> pulling your built tweetback repo the files are there, and the URLs being requested look correct. I think this is a case of falling victim to GitHub Pages / Jekyll not serving the _pagefind directory, due to the leading underscore.</p><p>One option would be to configure your Jekyll to include the _pagefind dir. Alternatively, configure Pagefind's bundle-dir to just \"pagefind\". Or migrate Pagefind to 1.0 😄</p><p>(This is the reason Pagefind 1.0 changed from \"_pagefind\" to \"pagefind\")</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112129703534202457",
			"parentId": "112129693956619809",
			"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@bglw/112129703054488561",
			"author": {
				"name": "Liam Bigelow",
				"url": "https://fosstodon.org/@bglw",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/343/937/842/242/416/original/8905219901a4a606.jpeg"
			},
			"date": "2024-03-20T19:38:06.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> <span class=\"h-card\" translate=\"no\"><a href=\"https://social.treehouse.systems/@egallager\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>egallager</span></a></span> </p><p>Jekyll include config: <a href=\"https://jekyllrb.com/docs/configuration/options/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" translate=\"no\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">jekyllrb.com/docs/configuratio</span><span class=\"invisible\">n/options/</span></a></p><p>If changing the directory, Pagefind 0.x uses the --bundle-dir flag. If that's hard to set through tweetback, you can also set a `PAGEFIND_BUNDLE_DIR=\"pagefind\"` environment variable and it'll pick that up.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "112133714442626070",
			"parentId": "112129703534202457",
			"url": "https://social.treehouse.systems/@egallager/112133714399216159",
